Q: C++ references in `extern &quot;C&quot;` A: <p>The compiler will enforce some restrictions on extern &quot;C&quot; functions. For example, if you try to declare overload functions:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>extern &quot;C&quot; {
    void f(int);
    void f(double);
}
</code></pre>
<p>...the compiler will normally issue an error message. For example, gcc says:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;source&gt;:3:10: error: conflicting declaration of C function 'void f(double)'
note: previous declaration 'void f(int)'
</code></pre>
<p>...but it doesn't enforce <em>all</em> the restrictions necessary to assure that the function is compatible with C. So although it tries to stop some ways of shooting yourself in the foot, you can't rely on it to do so in every case.</p>

Q: Java Segment Tree with Lazy Propagation getting Time Limit Exceeded on CSES *Range Updates and Sums* A: <p>Edit:<br />
I've modified the parameters of the inner <em>for</em> loop so that it only iterates over a specific “section” of the array. this not only limits the number of iterations but also eliminates the need for “range” checks; depending on the input, the difference can range from negligible to enormous (if it’s not too much trouble, I’d like you to test it on the platform where it was published to see “how poorly” it performs):</p>
<p>I don't know what led you to use that approach; I don't think it's the right one. Here's an alternative that works, though I can't verify whether its performance matches expectations:</p>
<pre><code>public class Cocho implements Mensajera {

     // according to the description, we receive a string like this
   int input[][] = {

        // array length and number of operations
      { 6, 5 },

        // array to be processed
      { 2, 3, 1, 1, 5, 3 },

        // the operations
      { 3, 3, 5 },
      { 1, 2, 4, 2 },
      { 3, 3, 5 },
      { 2, 2, 4, 5 },
      { 3, 3, 5 }
   };

   public void cochear( int input[][] ) {
      int len = input[ 0 ][ 0 ],
          operations = input[ 0 ][ 1 ],
          arr[] = input[ 1 ];

      List&lt;Integer&gt; results = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

      for( int k = 2; k &lt; operations + 2; k ++ ) {
         int sum = 0,

                // he following lines can be omitted, I
                // included them to make the code more readable.
             type = input[ k ][ 0 ],
             init = input[ k ][ 1 ] - 2,
             fin = input[ k ][ 2 ];

         boolean adding = false;
         if( type == 3 ) adding = true;


           // depending on the type of operation, we determine
           // whether it falls within the parameters, and then
           // proceed to perform that operation
         for( int i = init + 1; i &lt; fin; i ++ ) {
            if( type == 1 ) {
               arr[ i ] += input[ k ][ 3 ];
            }
            else if( type == 2 ) {
               arr[ i ] = input[ k ][ 3 ];
            }
            else if( type == 3 ) {
               sum += arr[ i ];
            }
         }
         if( adding ) {
            results.add( sum );
         }
      }
      System.out.println( results );
   }

   void ini() {
      cochear( input );
   }

   public static void main( String[] args ) {
      new Cocho().ini();
   }
}
</code></pre>
<p>I hope this serves as a guide for you.</p>

